<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daochus II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Daochos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daochus II was a nobleman of Pharsalus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> as tetrarch of Thessaly and represented the Thessalians on the Amphictyonic Council at Delphi. Around 336–332 BCE he set up at Delphi a group of marble statues of his family, the Daochus Monument, which includes the athlete Agias and is thought to echo a bronze by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a>.</p></div>
